# Question: when a SIGHUP reload removes ssh from an account's login-service
# set, does an SSH session opened BEFORE the reload keep answering commands
# while a NEW SSH login is refused and REST stays admitted?
# Why a unit test cannot decide it: proving SIGHUP reload tightens new logins
# without severing already-established SSH sessions requires a live daemon, a
# persistent SSH TCP session, and a real SIGHUP config reload.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-5, AC-13 -- reload closes door for new logins while open SSH session survives
# PREVENTS: reload failing to apply tightened login-service sets or wrongly killing open SSH sessions

option=timeout:value=60s
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture plugin/login-service-reload-tightens $PORT $PORT2:timeout=50s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=OK: pre-reload SSH connection opened and answered
expect=stderr:contains=OK: already-open SSH connection kept answering after reload
expect=stderr:contains=OK: new SSH login refused after reload withdrew ssh
expect=stderr:contains=OK: REST remained admitted after reload tightened SSH
reject=stderr:contains=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
